# BA degree completion: which pair of races carries the factor -0.9568?

Data: `data/nls_ses_processed.csv` (8773 rows, 0 missing, 236 BA events).
`BA DEGREE COMPLETED` stored as True/False -> mapped to 1/0. Race = `SAMPLE_RACE`
(White 5337 / Black 2113 / Hispanic 1323). Dummy coding, reference = **White**.
Environment: python 3.12.14, pandas 3.0.2, numpy 2.4.4, statsmodels 0.14.6.

## A. Crude shares and pairwise odds ratios
| race | events | n | share % | odds |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| White | 189 | 5337 | 3.541 | 0.03671 |
| Black | 28 | 2113 | 1.325 | 0.01343 |
| Hispanic | 19 | 1323 | 1.436 | 0.01457 |

Crude log-OR: Black vs White -1.0057 (OR 0.366 [0.245,0.546], p=8.4e-07);
Hispanic vs White -0.9241 (OR 0.397 [0.247,0.639], p=1.4e-04);
Black vs Hispanic -0.0816 (OR 0.922 [0.513,1.657], p=0.79).

## B. Primary specification: logit(BA ~ SAMPLE_RACE), base = White
| term | coef | SE | z | p | 95% CI | OR [CI] |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Intercept | -3.3046 | 0.0741 | -44.62 | <1e-16 | [-3.450,-3.160] | 0.037 |
| Black vs White | **-1.0057** | 0.2042 | -4.93 | 8.4e-07 | [-1.406,-0.606] | 0.366 [0.245,0.546] |
| Hispanic vs White | **-0.9241** | 0.2427 | -3.81 | 1.4e-04 | [-1.400,-0.449] | 0.397 [0.247,0.639] |

n=8773, converged (9 iters), EPV=78.7, AIC 2136.72, BIC 2157.96.
Neither coefficient equals -0.9568; the target lies *between* them.

## C. Specification reproducing the target: logit(BA ~ SAMPLE_RACE * SES), base = White
SES in the file is an approximately standardized composite: mean -0.0090, sd 0.7424,
range [-2.4455, 2.7182], quartiles -0.4782 / -0.0795 / +0.4129. Hence SES = 0 is
essentially the sample-mean SES (not zero SES).

| term | coef | SE | z | p |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Intercept | -3.3549 | 0.0794 | -42.24 | <1e-16 |
| **Black vs White (at SES = 0)** | **-0.9568** | 0.2154 | -4.44 | 8.8e-06 |
| Hispanic vs White (at SES = 0) | -0.7160 | 0.2449 | -2.92 | 0.0035 |
| SES | 0.2169 | 0.1003 | 2.16 | 0.031 |
| Black:SES | 0.7606 | 0.2545 | 2.99 | 0.0028 |
| Hispanic:SES | 0.4555 | 0.2877 | 1.58 | 0.113 |

Exact value -0.956778 -> -0.9568 at 4 dp (|diff| = 2.2e-05). Interpretation: the
log-odds ratio for **Black vs White evaluated at SES = 0** (a contrast at a fixed SES,
NOT the sample-average race effect); OR = exp(-0.9568) = 0.384, i.e. about 62 % lower
odds of completing a BA.

## D. Robustness
Black-vs-White contrast from the interaction model at SES quantiles
(q25 -1.3205 OR 0.267; q50 -1.0173 OR 0.362; q75 -0.6427 OR 0.526); ATC for Black
respondents at their own mean SES (-0.2095): log-OR -1.1161 (SE 0.2370), OR 0.328,
probability-scale ATC -2.00 pp [-2.75,-1.22] vs crude -2.22 pp.
Black-vs-White is negative and significant everywhere from SES = -2.45 up to about
SES = +1.0 (sign change at SES = 1.26, inside the range but at the 92nd percentile,
few Black respondents there). Hispanic-vs-White is consistently smaller in magnitude
than Black-vs-White at every SES level.
Model comparison (n = 8773): race only AIC 2136.72 / BIC 2157.96; race+SES
AIC 2123.30 / BIC 2151.62; race*SES AIC 2117.73 / BIC 2160.21
(LR chi2(2)=9.574, p=0.0083: interaction supported by AIC/LR, penalised by BIC).

## E. Rare-event diagnostics
EPV: 78.7 (race only), 59.0 (race+SES), 39.3 (race*SES) - all >= 10, all fits converged
in 9 Newton iterations, tolerance 1e-12, no separation warnings at these specifications.
Binding constraint is the small number of BA completions (Black 28, Hispanic 19).
Firth penalized refits: race-only Black -0.9908 / Hispanic -0.9011 (very close to MLE);
race*SES Black main effect -0.9317 (|diff| to target 0.025 - the target is an MLE value,
not a Firth value). Ridge (intercept unpenalized): lam=1 race-only Black -0.9614,
Hispanic -0.8687; lam=5 Black -0.8237. Conclusion: Black-vs-White is about -1.0 and
stable; the exact -0.9568 is specific to the unpenalized interaction MLE.

## F. Which pair? (uniqueness)
Grid of 42 formulas x 3 reference categories (all covariate subsets of SES, ability,
class percentile, family size, sex; race x covariate interactions) = 252 race terms.
Exactly ONE term rounds to -0.9568: `Rb[T.Black]` in `logit(BA ~ SAMPLE_RACE * SES)`
with reference White -> pair = **Black vs White**. Closest Hispanic-vs-White term
anywhere in the grid: -0.9284 (|diff| 0.028), so the Hispanic-White reading is rejected.

## Answer
The factor -0.9568 is obtained when **Black** respondents are compared with **White**
respondents (White = reference): Black respondents have exp(-0.9568) = 0.384 times the
odds of completing a BA at SES = 0 in the race x SES logit model; unadjusted the
Black-vs-White log-OR is -1.0057 (OR 0.366), Hispanic-vs-White -0.9241 (OR 0.397).
